⸻

The Correct Mental Model

Do not think:

User login lives inside a worker.
Worker stops.
Everything stops.
User logs in again tomorrow.

Think:

User workspace owns channel connection metadata.
Gateway owns live channel runtime.
Session state is persisted according to placement policy.
Heavy worker wakes only when needed.
Control plane owns policy, audit, approval, billing, registry.

Correct lifecycle:

User connects Telegram once.
Gateway creates local Telegram session state.
Session state is persisted.
Gateway listens or reconnects.
Agent worker is started only when needed.
Worker uses gateway to act.
Worker stops.
Gateway and/or session state survives.

⸻

Core Architecture Rule

Agent worker can die.
Gateway should not die during always-on mode.
Gateway can die.
Login/session should not die if persisted correctly.
VPS/container can die.
Live answering stops until another gateway starts, but login/session can survive if state exists outside the destroyed runtime.
No running gateway = no real-time answering.
Persistent session is not the same as live presence.

This rule must be written into docs and tests.

